\chapter{Mode-I Benchmark and Fixed-Mesh Reference}
\label{ch:mode1_reference}

\section{Geometry and material parameters}

The benchmark is a $1.0\,\mathrm{mm}\times1.0\,\mathrm{mm}$ square plate containing a sharp, zero-gap horizontal crack of length $a_0=0.5\,\mathrm{mm}$ along $y=0.5\,\mathrm{mm}$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:mode1_geometry}). The zero-gap seam is essential: it represents the published sharp edge crack without introducing the compliance of a finite-width notch.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.52\textwidth]{fig_mode1_geometry.png}
  \caption{Mode-I benchmark geometry. The publication supports vertical loading and lower-edge support. The rigid-body restraint and any additional lateral constraints must be identified separately as reconstruction choices.}
  \label{fig:mode1_geometry}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Material and phase-field parameters used for the Mode-I benchmark \citep{pandey2025}.}
\label{tab:mode1_parameters}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Quantity & Symbol & Value \\
\midrule
Young's modulus & $E$ & $210\,\mathrm{GPa}$ \\
Poisson's ratio & $\nu$ & $0.3$ \\
Critical energy-release rate & $G_c$ & $2.7\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{kN/mm}$ \\
Regularization length & $l_0$ & $0.0075\,\mathrm{mm}$ \\
Residual stiffness parameter & $k_{\mathrm{res}}$ & $1.0\times 10^{-7}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Boundary-condition provenance}

Pandey and Kumar specify Mode-I displacement loading and lower-edge support in the benchmark description and figure \citep{pandey2025}. The project reconstruction constrains vertical motion on the lower edge and removes horizontal rigid-body translation at one point. A uniform top-edge $u_x=0$ constraint appears in project models, but it is a reconstruction choice and is not presented here as a condition explicitly reported by the publication. This distinction is retained because boundary conditions influence both the mechanical response and the elastic stress field that drives pre-refinement.

\section{Verified fixed-mesh response}

The reference calculation, Job \texttt{1398090}, uses 15,192 finite elements. It provides the response anchor against which the corrected adaptive calculation is compared. The peak reaction force is $F_{\max}=0.757778\,\mathrm{kN}$ at $u(F_{\max})=0.005857\,\mathrm{mm}$.

Initial stiffness is defined in this project by an unconstrained ordinary-least-squares fit with intercept over the first 400 active increments:
\begin{equation}
F_i=K_0u_i+b+\epsilon_i,
\qquad
K_0=137.945520\,\mathrm{kN/mm},
\label{eq:k0_fit}
\end{equation}
with $b=4.472368\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{kN}$ and $R^2=0.99999960$. Pandey and Kumar do not report $K_0$; it is a project-derived diagnostic and must not be attributed to the paper.

