\end{table}

\section{Boundary-set formatting defect and correction}

The first complete 71,320-element fracture deck contained a preprocessing defect in \texttt{N\_BOTTOM}. The intended set contained 150 nodes, but all identifiers were written on one malformed data line. Abaqus retained only the first 16 identifiers, so 134 intended bottom nodes were not constrained vertically. The resulting bottom-edge motion introduced artificial compliance and altered the fracture response.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.94\textwidth]{fig_nbottom_lift.png}
  \caption{Measured bottom-edge motion in the defective and corrected decks. Correct line wrapping restores all 150 intended vertical constraints and eliminates the artificial lift.}
  \label{fig:nbottom_lift}
\end{figure}

The correction is purely syntactic: node-set data are wrapped across valid input lines. No constitutive or remeshing parameter is changed. Table~\ref{tab:nbottom_comparison} shows the resulting recovery.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Mechanical effect of the \texttt{N\_BOTTOM} defect and its correction.}
\label{tab:nbottom_comparison}
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{Yrrrr}
\toprule
Case & Finite elements & $K_0$ [kN/mm] & $F_{\max}$ [kN] & $u(F_{\max})$ [mm] \\
\midrule
Fixed reference, Job \texttt{1398090} & 15,192 & 137.945520 & 0.757778 & 0.005857 \\
Defective diagnostic, Job \texttt{1399632} & 71,320 & $\approx122.38$ & 0.478203 & 0.004150 \\
Corrected full fracture, Job \texttt{1404933} & 71,320 & 137.820804 & 0.745325 & 0.005750 \\
Independent elastic requalification, Job \texttt{1405044} & 71,320 & 138.021013 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\clearpage
\begin{figure}[!htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.86\textwidth]{fig_mode1_fu_recovery.pdf}
  \caption{Force--displacement response of the fixed reference, the defective diagnostic case, and the corrected nominal 1\% reconstruction. The elastic-range agreement after repair demonstrates that the stiffness anomaly was an input-format defect, not an adaptive-mesh effect.}
  \label{fig:mode1_fu_recovery}
\end{figure}

The corrected adaptive result recovers the reference stiffness to within approximately $0.09\%$. Its peak force remains about $1.64\%$ below the fixed reference, so complete curve, field, temporal, and energy comparisons remain necessary before a general convergence conclusion is drawn.
